---
// Pulse privacy policy (app, waitlist and this website).
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/pulse.css';
import { site, mailto } from '../../config/site';
const email = site.contact.email;
const mail = mailto(email, 'Pulse privacy');
---
<Base title="Pulse privacy policy" description="How the Pulse app, the Pulse beta waitlist and this website use your information." image="/og/pulse.png">
  <div class="pulse">
    <article class="container p-page">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pulse</span>
        <h1>Pulse privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="muted">Last updated: 22 November 2026</p>

        <p>
          Pulse ("the app") is a revision app for medical graduates and interns, made by Nifty Games Studios ("we").
          This page explains what information the app, the Pulse beta waitlist and this website use, and why.
          You can reach us at <a href={mail}>{email}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>1. The beta waitlist</h2>
        <p>When you join the waitlist we collect:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>your name and email address;</li>
          <li>your Google account email, used only to add you to Google Play testing so you can install the beta;</li>
          <li>your stage (intern or PG aspirant) and exam year;</li>
          <li>which creator link you came from, if any.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The form is run by Tally, a form service, which stores the answers for us. We use them only to invite you to the
          Pulse beta and to send you beta updates. To invite you, we add your Google account email to the beta testers list
          in Google Play.
        </p>
        <p>
          We keep this information until the beta ends, or until you ask us to delete it, whichever comes first.
          To ask, email <a href={mail}>{email}</a> from the address you signed up with.
        </p>
        <p>If you follow our WhatsApp Channel for updates, you can leave it at any time.</p>

        <h2>2. This website</h2>
        <p>
          This website uses Google Analytics to see which pages are useful, and only after you allow it in the cookie notice.
          If you decline, no analytics cookies are set. You can change your choice at any time with "Cookie settings" at the
          bottom of every page. If you came from a creator's link, that link's short code is kept in your browser tab only
          while you visit and is passed to the waitlist form.
        </p>

        <h2>3. What stays on your phone</h2>
        <p>
          Your learning progress (cards you have read, your answers, XP, streak, achievements, journeys, favourites, settings and
          reminder times) is stored on your phone. We do not receive it unless you turn on backup (section 7).
        </p>

        <h2>4. Usage and crash data (only if you allow it)</h2>
        <p>
          On first launch the app asks whether you allow anonymous usage data. If you say yes, we use Google Firebase Analytics
          and Firebase Crashlytics to learn how the app is used and to fix crashes. If you say no, nothing is sent. You can
          change your mind at any time in Settings.
        </p>
        <table class="p-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Events such as "session finished", counts, and how long answers take</td><td>See which features work and where learners get stuck</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crash reports: device model, Android version, what the app was doing</td><td>Find and fix bugs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A random installation ID created by Firebase</td><td>Count how many people use the app without knowing who they are</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          We never send the text of cards or questions, or your answers, in these events. We do not use the advertising ID,
          we show no ads and we do not sell your data. Google processes this data for us under Firebase's terms, and we ask
          Firebase to keep it for the shortest period offered.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Downloading content</h2>
        <p>
          To download study content (cards and questions), the app signs in to Firebase Authentication anonymously. This
          creates a random ID that is not linked to your name, email or phone number, and it is needed whatever you chose in
          section 4. The app then reads the content from Google Firebase (Firestore). Your learning progress is not sent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some subjects include photographs, such as clinical photos with their credit lines. They are downloaded from a
          private image library hosted on GitHub. Like any website, GitHub can see your phone's internet address when a photo
          is requested. No account details or progress are sent.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Notifications</h2>
        <p>If you allow them, the app schedules reminders on your phone. They are created on the device and do not use a server.</p>

        <h2>7. Sign-in, backup and friends</h2>
        <p>
          You can sign in with Google. We then store, in Google Firebase (Firestore), your Google account email and name, a
          user ID, and (if you press "Back up now") a copy of your learning progress. Signing in and backing up are optional;
          the app works without them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Friends and the weekly leaderboard are a separate choice that you turn on yourself on the Friends screen. Only then
          do we publish a profile: the display name and avatar you chose, your level, total XP, XP this week, streak and up to
          three achievement badges you pin, plus a friend code. This profile is visible only to you and to the people you
          accept as friends, and a friend request shows the sender's display name and avatar to the person they ask. Friends
          never see your email, your cards or your answers. You can turn friends off at any time, and the profile, code and
          friendships are deleted.
        </p>

        <h2>8. How long we keep data and how to delete it</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Phone data:</strong> clear the app's storage, uninstall the app, or use "Reset all progress" in Settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Analytics and crash data:</strong> kept by Firebase for a limited period and then deleted automatically.</li>
          <li><strong>Account, profile and backup:</strong> delete them in the app ("Delete account", when available) or email <a href={mail}>{email}</a> from the address you signed in with. We delete them within 30 days.</li>
          <li><strong>Waitlist answers:</strong> kept until the beta ends, or deleted sooner when you ask by email.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>9. Who it is shared with</h2>
        <p>
          Only the services that process data for us: Google (Firebase, Google Play testing and, if you allow it, Google
          Analytics on this website) and Tally for the waitlist form. We do not sell data and we do not share it with
          advertisers or data brokers. Data sent from the app is encrypted in transit.
        </p>

        <h2>10. Age</h2>
        <p>Pulse is meant for adults (18 and over), such as medical graduates and doctors. It is not directed at children.</p>

        <h2>11. Medical disclaimer</h2>
        <p>
          Pulse is for exam revision only. It is not medical advice and must not be used to diagnose or treat patients.
          Always check your standard textbooks.
        </p>

        <h2>12. Your choices and contact</h2>
        <p>
          We respect your choices. You can ask us to show, correct or delete the data we hold about you at any time.
          Write to <a href={mail}>{email}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>13. Changes</h2>
        <p>If we change this policy we will update the date above and, for important changes, tell you in the app.</p>

        <p style="margin-top:2rem"><a href="/pulse/">Back to Pulse</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
